---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE, EDITORS } from '../consts';
import { breadcrumbSchema, graph, organizationSchema } from '../lib/seo';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'About', href: '/about/' },
];

const schema = graph([
  organizationSchema(),
  breadcrumbSchema(breadcrumbs),
  {
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: `About ${SITE.name}`,
    url: `${SITE.url}/about/`,
    isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#website` },
  },
]);
---

<PageLayout
  title="About HostScope"
  description="Who we are, why we publish renewal prices when most hosting sites do not, and how the site is funded."
  eyebrow="About"
  canonical={`${SITE.url}/about/`}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  schema={schema}
  updated={new Date('2026-09-10')}
>
  <p class="lead text-[1.125rem] text-ink-700">
    HostScope is an independent research site about Hostinger. We publish the numbers that make
    hosting decisions easy and that most comparison sites leave out — the renewal price, the resource
    ceilings, the backup frequency, and the plans you should not buy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why this site exists</h2>

  <p>
    Hosting reviews have a credibility problem. Search for any host and you will find dozens of pages
    that quote the introductory price, list the same six marketing bullets, and declare the host
    "the best" without saying who it is best for. Almost none of them tell you what the plan renews
    at, which is the number that determines what you actually spend.
  </p>

  <p>
    We started HostScope because we kept having the same conversation with friends and clients: the
    plan was fine, the price was not what they expected in year two. Hostinger is a genuinely good
    host for a lot of people — we recommend it — but it is only good value if you understand the
    term structure and pick the tier that matches your workload.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-funded">How the site is funded</h2>

  <p>
    HostScope earns money through the Hostinger affiliate programme. When someone buys hosting
    through one of our links, Hostinger pays us a commission. That commission is paid by Hostinger,
    not by you, and it does not change the price you pay.
  </p>

  <p>
    We think affiliate funding is fine, as long as three rules hold, and we hold to all three:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Disclosure is visible, not buried.</strong> Every monetised page carries a disclosure at
      the top. The full policy is on the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure page</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Commission never changes a recommendation.</strong> Hostinger's affiliate payouts do not
      vary enough between tiers to make upselling worthwhile. We recommend Premium for people who
      should buy Premium and we tell people not to buy Hostinger at all when a competitor fits better
      — our <a href="/compare/">comparison pages</a> award the win to competitors on plenty of rows.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We publish the bad numbers.</strong> The renewal increase on every plan, the weekly-only
      backups on Premium, the absence of Node.js support on the cheapest tier, the 10-emails-per-minute
      cap on server-based sending. If we hid these, nobody would trust the good numbers.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="team">Who writes this</h2>

  <p>
    Three people. Between us we have migrated several hundred sites between hosts, run client
    infrastructure on KVM boxes, and built and broken enough WooCommerce stores to know where the
    resource ceilings actually are.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
    {
      Object.entries(EDITORS).map(([key, editor]) => {
        const hue = { 'maya-ross': '#673DE6', 'daniel-okafor': '#0E7490', 'priya-nair': '#B45309' }[
          key
        ];
        const initials = editor.name
          .split(' ')
          .map((p) => p[0])
          .join('');
        return (
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-5">
            <span
              class="flex h-12 w-12 items-center justify-center rounded-full text-[0.9375rem] font-bold text-white"
              style={`background:linear-gradient(135deg, ${hue}, ${hue}cc)`}
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              {initials}
            </span>
            <p class="mt-3 text-[0.9375rem] font-bold text-ink-900">{editor.name}</p>
            <p class="text-[0.75rem] font-semibold text-brand-700">{editor.role}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-600">{editor.bio}</p>
          </div>
        );
      })
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="not-affiliated">What we are not</h2>

  <p>
    HostScope is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or operated by Hostinger International Ltd. We are
    an independent publication. Hostinger does not review our articles before publication, does not
    have input into our scores, and cannot ask us to remove criticism. Our contact at Hostinger is the
    same affiliate programme contact available to anyone.
  </p>

  <p>
    We also do not run paid placements, sponsored posts or "featured partner" slots. If a page
    mentions another host, it is because the comparison is useful, not because that host paid.
  </p>

  <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>

  <p>
    Hosting prices and specs change constantly. Every page that quotes a price records the date it
    was checked — currently <strong>{SITE.pricingVerified}</strong>. If you spot something that is out
    of date or wrong, email{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.publisher.email}`}>{SITE.publisher.email}</a> and we will fix it and note
    the correction. Getting a number wrong is worse for us than admitting it.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
